id: pm-uenmu9
title: Restore accepted manual same-day follow-up releases while preserving one automatic release per UTC day
description: "The accepted calendar-versioning ADR pm-ee1k already defines YYYY.M.D for the first UTC-day release, YYYY.M.D-N for explicit manual follow-ups, and at most one automatic release per day. The current pipeline drifted from that decision: its same-day guard is trigger-blind and its resolver rejects the ordinal version, so this item now owns restoring the accepted behavior, enumerating every version consumer, and proving automatic triggers cannot consume the manual exception."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 2
tags[4]: ci,policy,release,versioning
created_at: "2026-07-31T14:13:07.836Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-30T11:30:58.412Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 120
acceptance_criteria: "A scheduled or issue-triggered automatic path can publish at most one version for a UTC date, with a negative control that a second automatic attempt skips before version mutation; An explicit workflow_dispatch after the daily release can mint the next stable YYYY.M.D-N follow-up and records immutable manual-trigger provenance; Every version parser, comparator, synchronizer, changelog grouper, package range, plugin manifest, release verifier, npm check, npx probe, and bunx probe is inventoried and covered for base and ordinal forms; Concurrent manual dispatches allocate one ordinal without collision or duplicate publication; Partial-release recovery distinguishes retrying the existing version from requesting a distinct follow-up; docs/RELEASING.md states the two paths and their exact receipts; focused tests plus the full local and hosted release gates prove npm, npx, Bun, and bunx behavior without publishing during validation"
parent: pm-u9d0
risk: medium
confidence: 92
